Hello,

As Olsin mentioned, this file is already compressed (the "z" in
"vmzcore" implies this).  If you want to un-compress it, use
"expand_dump" as Olsin also mentioned.
The same applies for using "dbx" to analyze the dump. :-)  It's not
often that you need to un-compress a vmzcore file though, so before
doing that, see my comments below.

Since this is a dump of the running system (and not an application
core dump, for example), I would recommend using the syntax "dbx -k
vmunix.28 vmzcore.28" (the "-k" says that you are evaluating a kernel
image).

Before doing that though, you may want to simply look through the
"crash-data.28" file; this is an ascii-readable file that provides a
thorough summary (oftentimes) of what happened at the time of the
crash.  You will get a panic string in there, as well as a stack trace
that shows the routines leading to panic (among other things).

Hope this helps,

--John Lanier



