Hello,

I have seen similiar behavior when performing similiar tasks, and this
can sometimes be a symptom of either device special file or hardware
database corruption.  Here is what I would recommend to try and fix
this:

1=2E  hwmgr view dev (any devices reporting "null"?)
2=2E  hwmgr sh scsi (any devices reporting "null"? If so, remove them
per #7 below)
3=2E  dsfmgr -vV (any errors?)
4=2E  dsfmgr -vFV (if any errors seen in #3)
5=2E  dsfmgr -vV (any errors?  If not, try labelling the disk; if you
can't, go to #6)
6=2E  hwmgr show scsi (note the "scsi did" of dsk10)
7=2E  hwmgr del scsi -did scsi_did_of_dsk10
8=2E  hwmgr scan scsi (scans the scsi bus & registers devices that
aren't registered)
9=2E  hwmgr show scsi (should show a new device in place of "dsk10")
10. disklabel -r new_dskN (does it still fail with "bad file number"?
If it says "disk
      is un-labelled", then the disk is simply un-labelled at this
point)
11. if #10 was successful, you can move the "new" name back to the
"old" name
     via "dsfmgr -m newname oldname" then write a label via "disklabel
-rw dskN".


Hope this helps,

--John Lanier



